h1="DHA Phase 1 Lahore — Tax Help for Inherited Homes, Retired Owners and Their Families",
lead="Phase 1 is one of DHA Lahore's earliest phases, and many of its houses have been in the same family for decades. That history shapes the tax questions we get from Phase 1: a father has passed away and the house must move to his heirs, a retired officer is drawing a pension and some rent, or children abroad want to know whether they need to file anything for their share.",
body="""
## Inheriting a Phase 1 house: what is and is not taxed
Pakistan has no inheritance tax. When a house passes to legal heirs, nobody pays income tax simply because they inherited it, and DHA's transfer of the property to heirs under a succession certificate or letters of administration is not a sale. That is the good news, and it is where many families stop thinking about tax.

The obligations that remain are about declaration, not payment:
- Each heir who files a return must show their share of the house in their wealth statement, from the date of inheritance, with "inheritance" as the source.
- The value used is normally the value at which it was inherited, which also becomes the starting point if that heir later sells.
- If the house is rented out after inheritance, each heir's share of rent is income in their own return, unless the heirs choose to manage the property jointly as an association of persons.

## The sibling problem
A common Phase 1 situation: three siblings inherit, one lives in the house, two live abroad, and nobody has decided whether to sell. Until the property is sold or partitioned, each sibling owns an undivided share. The sibling in Pakistan usually needs to file anyway; the overseas siblings may or may not, depending on their residency status and whether they have Pakistan-source income such as their share of rent.

When the house is eventually sold, the seller's advance tax under Section 236C is collected at transfer — at a lower rate for sellers on the Active Taxpayer List. An overseas sibling who is not on the ATL pays the higher rate on their share. Getting each sibling onto the ATL before the sale is usually cheaper than paying the difference, and we plan that timing with families months ahead. Our guide on [declaring inherited wealth](/blogs/inheritance-wealth-declaration-pakistan) covers the paperwork in more detail.

## Retired owners: pension, rent and the wealth statement
Many Phase 1 homeowners are retired from government, the armed forces or large companies. Their tax position is usually simple but easy to get slightly wrong:
- Pension received from the government or an approved scheme is treated differently from salary, and the treatment depends on the source — we check this line by line rather than assuming it is exempt.
- Rent from an annexe, upper portion or a second property is taxed under its own head, and a tenant that is a company or institution is required to withhold tax from the rent.
- Bank profit on savings, often a significant part of a retiree's income, has tax deducted by the bank at a higher rate for non-filers, which alone can justify filing.

> Many retired Phase 1 owners stopped filing years ago because "there was no salary". Their banks have since deducted the higher non-filer rate on every profit payment. Filing again — and getting back on the ATL — often stops that leakage immediately.

## Rebuilding a long-dormant file
For owners who have not filed for several years, we start by checking the IRIS record: is the NTN active, which years show as missing, and what advance tax has been deducted in the meantime. We then file the current year with a full wealth statement, and look at whether earlier years need to be filed to support the opening wealth figures. The aim is a single clean position that the next sale or inheritance can build on.

## Documents worth digging out
- DHA allotment or transfer letter and the date the property came to the family
- Succession certificate or letters of administration, if the owner has passed away
- Pension statements and the bank statements where pension and rent are received
- Tenancy agreements for any rented portion
- CNICs of all heirs, and passport details for those living abroad
""",
