h1="DHA Phase 2 Lahore — A Landlord's Guide to Declaring Rental Income Properly",
lead="A large share of the Phase 2 clients who contact us are landlords: owners who live elsewhere in DHA or abroad and rent out a whole house, or families who have let the upper floor or an annexe. Rent is one of the easiest incomes for FBR to trace, so this is where we see avoidable notices.",
body="""
## Why rent shows up even when you don't declare it
Rent in DHA rarely moves in cash anymore. It arrives by cheque or bank transfer, often from a company paying for an executive's housing. Corporate tenants are legally required to withhold tax from rent and deposit it against the landlord's CNIC, which means the rent appears in the landlord's FBR profile whether or not the landlord files. Tenancy agreements also pass through DHA when a tenant needs permission or a police verification. A landlord who leaves rent out of the return is not hiding anything from FBR — they are just creating a mismatch.

## How rental income is taxed
Rent from property is taxed as its own head of income, separate from salary or business. In practice that means:
- Your rental income is worked out on its own, not simply added to your salary.
- Rent received in advance, and non-refundable deposits, can count as income — we check the agreement's wording.
- Where a company tenant has withheld tax, that deduction is credited against the tax due on the rent.
- Co-owners each report their own share; a husband and wife who own a Phase 2 house jointly do not both declare the full rent.

Because the rules on rate and deductions have changed several times in recent budgets, we compute the tax on the actual figures for the year you are filing rather than relying on an old rule of thumb. Our [rental income tax guide](/blogs/rental-income-tax-guide-pakistan-2026) explains the current treatment.

## Expenses landlords forget to claim — and ones they shouldn't
| Usually claimable | Usually not claimable |
| Repairs within the allowed limit | Your own household running costs |
| Property tax paid to Excise on the rented house | Cost of building an extra floor (capital, not expense) |
| Insurance premium on the building | Furniture for your own portion |
| Ground rent or society charges payable by owner | Personal loan interest unrelated to the property |

The allowed deductions have their own limits and conditions, so keep receipts. We would rather claim a smaller amount that survives scrutiny than a large one that triggers a query.

## Renting out part of the house you live in
Many Phase 2 families let the upper portion while living downstairs. The rent is still rental income, but it also changes your wealth statement in small ways: the security deposit you hold is a liability you owe back to the tenant, and if the portion was built or renovated for letting, the cost belongs in the property's value. Leaving the deposit out is a surprisingly common reason for a reconciliation that is "off" by a few lakh.

> If you own the house jointly with your spouse, check whose name the tenancy agreement and bank account are in. Rent that lands in one spouse's account but belongs half to each needs to be shown that way in both returns.

## Owners living outside Lahore or Pakistan
Absentee landlords are common in Phase 2. If you live abroad, rent from a DHA house is Pakistan-source income and is taxable here whatever your residency status. You can file online through us without visiting; we only need the agreement, bank statement and your CNIC or NICOP.
""",
