h1="DHA Phase 8 Lahore — Tax Questions for Families Split Between Lahore and Abroad",
lead="Phase 8, on the airport side of DHA, is home to many households where at least one member works or studies overseas. These families often own the house, a car and investments in Lahore while the main income is earned abroad — a setup that raises residency questions most standard returns are not built for.",
body="""
## Resident or non-resident: the question that decides everything
An individual is generally a resident of Pakistan for a tax year if they are present here for 183 days or more in that year, with an additional test for people who spend shorter but regular periods in Pakistan over several years. A resident is taxed on worldwide income; a non-resident only on Pakistan-source income.

For a Phase 8 family, this usually means:
- The member working in the Gulf, UK or North America is often non-resident, and their foreign salary is outside Pakistani tax.
- The spouse and children living in the Lahore house are residents, and their own income (if any) is taxable here.
- Rent, bank profit and property sales in Pakistan are Pakistan-source income, whoever earns them.

Our page on the [non-resident status and day-count rules](/blogs/fbr-non-resident-status-182-day-rule-pakistan-2026) explains the tests in more detail. We count days from passport stamps and travel records, not estimates.

## Who in the family should file?
| Family member | Typical situation | Filing usually needed? |
| Earner abroad, non-resident | Owns the Phase 8 house, no Pakistani income | Often yes, to stay on ATL for property and banking |
| Spouse in Lahore | Receives remittances, no income of her own | Depends on assets held in her name |
| Spouse in Lahore with a job or business | Own salary or business income | Yes |
| Retired parents in the house | Pension and bank profit | Usually beneficial |

Remittances sent home through banking channels are not income of the person receiving them; they are transfers within the family. But if they fund assets in the receiving spouse's name — a car, gold, a plot — those assets need a visible source, which is usually a gift from the earner.

## Owning the Phase 8 house while living abroad
Non-residents can and often should file in Pakistan if they own property, because:
1. Advance tax on any future sale or purchase is lower for those on the Active Taxpayer List.
2. Banks deduct lower withholding on profit for filers.
3. A clean filing history makes it simpler to repatriate sale proceeds or prove the source of funds later.

A non-resident's return can be very short — Pakistan-source income only, plus a wealth statement of Pakistani assets — but it still needs to be accurate.

> Families sometimes keep the house in a parent's name "to keep things simple". It is worth checking whether that parent's return actually shows it; if not, the simplicity is only on paper.

## Coming back to Lahore for good
Returning residents need to plan the year of return: foreign savings brought back, foreign assets still held abroad, and the date residency resumes. We handle returning-resident filings regularly — see our guide for [returning overseas Pakistanis](/blogs/hire-tax-consultant-returning-overseas-pakistanis).
""",
