desc="Building or renovating in DHA Q Block? How to record construction cost in your wealth statement, document contractor payments and avoid reconciliation gaps.",
h1="DHA Q Block Lahore — Recording a House Build or Renovation So It Stands Up Later",
lead="In DHA's older blocks, Q Block among them, houses are regularly being rebuilt, extended or completely renovated. A rebuild can easily cost as much as a new plot, yet it is the expense people most often forget to put into their tax records. When the house is later sold or FBR asks how it was funded, the missing construction cost becomes a problem.",
body="""
## Construction cost belongs in the wealth statement
When you build, rebuild or substantially renovate, the money spent becomes part of the property's value. In your wealth statement:
- The house's value goes up by the construction cost incurred in each tax year.
- The funds used — salary, business income, savings, loan or gift — must account for that spending.
- If you sell later, the higher cost reduces your taxable gain.

Leave it out, and two things go wrong: the money spent disappears from your reconciliation (so your declared wealth looks lower than your income suggests), and when you sell, the gain looks larger than it really is.

## The difference between maintenance and improvement
| Type of spending | Treatment in your records |
| Rebuilding the house, adding a floor, major structural work | Capital — added to the property's cost |
| Full renovation that substantially upgrades the house | Generally capital |
| Repainting, routine repairs, replacing a broken fixture | Household expense |
| Furniture and appliances | Household assets or expense, not part of the building |

The line is not always obvious, and we look at the actual scope of work before deciding. Getting it right affects both this year's reconciliation and any future capital gain.

## Documenting contractor payments
Most construction in Lahore is still done through a contractor (thekedar) paid partly in cash. That is common, but it is weak evidence. To make your records hold up:
1. Have a written agreement with the contractor stating the scope and price, even a simple one.
2. Pay through bank transfer or cheque wherever possible.
3. Keep invoices from material suppliers — steel, cement, tiles, sanitary fittings.
4. Keep a simple running ledger of payments with dates.
5. Keep DHA's building plan approval and completion certificate.

Large cash withdrawals from your own account to pay the contractor are fine, as long as you can show where they went.

## Financing a rebuild
If you fund construction through a bank's home-finance or renovation facility, the loan appears as a liability in your wealth statement and reduces as you repay it. If family members contribute, document whether it is a gift or a loan — a loan needs to show up as a liability and eventually be repaid.

> We often meet Q Block owners who rebuilt five or six years ago and now want to sell. If the construction never appeared in their returns, the best course is usually to revise returns where possible or document the cost carefully before the sale, rather than accept tax on an overstated gain.

## Renting out the rebuilt house
If the new house or a portion of it is let out, rental income begins from the date of letting. See our [DHA Phase 2 landlord guide](/blogs/tax-consultant-dha-phase-2-lahore) for how rent is declared.
""",
