desc="Own or run a shop in DHA Y Block market? Retailer tax basics: sole proprietor return, FBR vs PRA sales tax, digital invoicing, and handling a shop's cash sales.",
h1="DHA Y Block Lahore — Tax Filing for Shopkeepers and Retailers in the Market",
lead="Y Block market in DHA Phase 3 is known for its food outlets, but it is also full of retail shops — pharmacies, boutiques, bakeries, electronics and grocery stores. Shopkeepers here tend to fall into one of two camps: those whose accountant handles everything, and those who have never filed because 'the shop is small'. This page is for the second camp.",
body="""
## Is a shop's income really taxable if it's small?
Yes, once the profit is above the taxable threshold — and the threshold applies to the owner's total income, not the shop's turnover. A shop with high turnover but thin margins may have modest taxable profit; a small boutique with good margins may owe more than its size suggests. Either way, the starting point is filing a return as a sole proprietor that shows sales, purchases, expenses and profit.

## Sales tax: goods vs services
Retail shops mostly sell goods, which fall under FBR's federal sales tax. Shops that sell services — salons, repair shops, food served on premises — fall under the Punjab Revenue Authority for sales tax on services. Some businesses do both.

| Business in Y Block | Sales tax authority (typical) |
| Pharmacy, grocery, clothing, electronics | FBR (goods) |
| Salon, tailoring service, repair counter | PRA (services) |
| Bakery selling over the counter | FBR (goods) |
| Café or dine-in food | PRA (restaurant services) |

Whether registration is required depends on the type of retailer and turnover; larger and "tier-1" retailers have specific obligations including integrated point-of-sale systems. We check where your shop falls before advising anything. See our guide to [FBR digital invoicing for small retailers](/blogs/fbr-digital-invoicing-cost-small-retailers-pakistan).

## Handling a cash-heavy shop honestly
Cash is normal in retail. The goal is not to eliminate it but to account for it:
1. Record daily sales — a simple register or POS, every day.
2. Deposit takings regularly so the bank statement reflects business activity.
3. Keep supplier invoices; your purchases are the biggest expense and the easiest to prove.
4. Separate personal withdrawals from business payments, even if the same account is used.
5. At year end, sales minus purchases, minus rent, salaries and utilities gives the profit you file.

## Rent, staff and the shop's other costs
Shop rent in DHA commercial areas is substantial and a genuine expense. So are staff salaries, electricity and maintenance charges. Claiming them properly can reduce taxable profit significantly — but only with documents: rent agreement and payment record, salary register, utility bills in the shop's or owner's name.

> Shopkeepers sometimes run the business in a family member's name to "keep it separate". If that person has no return, the shop's income has no owner on paper. Make sure whoever owns the shop on paper is the one filing for it.

## Starting without a back-log
If you have never filed, we normally register your NTN, file the current year as a sole proprietor with a wealth statement, and discuss whether previous years need attention. A shop that begins filing voluntarily is in a far better position than one discovered through an inspection or a bank report. Our [traders' tax filing guide](/tax-filing-traders-pakistan) covers the basics for most retail businesses.
""",
